      "Threat intelligence is information about who might attack you, how and why, collected and analysed so that you can make better decisions. Many small businesses assume it is only for large corporations, but modest, well-chosen intelligence can sharpen priorities and save money.",
      "## Start with what you have to protect",
      "Begin by identifying your most valuable assets, such as customer data, payment systems, intellectual property and key supplier relationships. Then ask who would want them and how they might be reached. A professional-services firm, for example, is more likely to face invoice fraud and credential theft than elaborate sabotage.",
      "Free and low-cost sources can help. The National Cyber Security Centre publishes guidance and alerts, and its Small Business Guide offers a practical baseline. Sector bodies, your bank and your insurer often share warnings about active scams, and the Cyber Essentials scheme is a useful framework for basic controls.",
      "## Turning information into action",
      "Intelligence is only useful if it changes something. When a new phishing lure is reported, update staff awareness. When a vulnerability in widely used software is announced, check whether you use it and patch promptly. When your domain or staff emails appear in a breach, force password changes and review multi-factor authentication.",
